1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
 After laser cutting, my next favorite manufacturing process is folding of sheet metal,

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:08,240
 and we're going to talk about that in this video, alongside bending and forming of metals in general.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:10,480
 Like many of the other processes that we're meeting this week,

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:14,160
 bending, folding, and forming can be applied to other materials as well.

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,120
 Though in this case, we're going to be focused on the application of these techniques to

6
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,360
 metals, particularly ductile metals.

7
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:23,200
 Bending, folding, and forming techniques can be used to very economically build up

8
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,080
 complicated geometries that are lightweight and simple and reasonably easy to manufacture.

9
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,360
 All of these techniques kind of work the same way, and a little bit like forging.

10
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:35,280
 You clamp a metal in one place and you impart some plastic deformation in another.

11
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:38,320
 The words folding and bending are typically used to describe techniques that

12
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,120
 plastically deform metals along straight lines.

13
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:44,800
 Folding is generally used to refer to working with thinner metals, with sheet metal,

14
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,080
 and bending is typically used to describe working with thicker metals.

15
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:52,000
 Whether or not you can fold or bend a metal is very much a function of its yield strength and its

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,240
 ductility. Generally speaking, it's easier to bend metals that have a lower yield strength,

17
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:59,760
 and your bends are much more likely to be successful if the material is ductile,

18
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:01,600
 otherwise they might snap or tear.

19
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,240
 You can't really bend very brittle materials, and extremely strong metals, irrespective of whether

20
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,840
 the ductile will require more and more power to be able to bend them.

21
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:14,240
 This power that you require to bend very strong metals normally manifests the machine doing

22
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,920
 the bending needing to be stronger and stiffer and thus more expensive.

23
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,520
 But generally speaking, if you're building a component out of mild steel or aluminium,

24
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:24,080
 as you more than likely will be in a lot of cases,

25
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:27,600
 bending and folding are very economical ways to build up complicated geometries

26
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,120
 and actually add effective strength and stiffness to your parts.

27
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:33,600
 You'll talk a little bit more about this in your later year design courses,

28
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:36,160
 but there are lots of different ways to achieve the same result.

29
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,160
 A strong stiff part can be made out of 6mm thick steel welded together,

30
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:44,480
 but can also be made out of 1mm thick steel with some folded or bent returns on it.

31
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:47,600
 In fact, you might be surprised to know that much of your car is made out of very,

32
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,720
 very thin sections of steel, 0.6 to 1mm steel.

33
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:53,600
 This is possible because the panels that make up your car have lots of bends

34
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,800
 and folds and formations in them that add stiffness to the part.

35
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,360
 Designing components to be bent or folded can be a little bit tricky

36
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:02,880
 and it's one of the things you're going to talk about in your later year design courses.

37
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,640
 You need to account for things like how much material is lost in the bend.

38
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:08,960
 You can probably imagine that as you bend a bit of material,

39
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,760
 the outside of it needs to grow and the inside of it needs to shrink

40
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:13,280
 just like if you're bending a beam.

41
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:15,440
 And consequently, when laying out a component to be bent,

42
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:18,960
 you need to leave enough material for a bend radius to exist.

43
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,320
 The amount of space that you need to leave for a bend radius is very much a function

44
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:23,680
 of the material you're dealing with.

45
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,400
 Some materials are more likely to plastically to form when being bent

46
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:28,160
 and so you need to leave less than others.

47
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,120
 Thankfully, modding CAD package to make designs for foldable bent components

48
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:32,400
 relatively straightforward.

49
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,120
 You can design the component you want in the geometry that you'd like

50
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:38,080
 and then unfold the components by telling the CAD package

51
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:39,760
 what material you're working with.

52
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:42,320
 Again, you'll touch on this more in your later year design courses,

53
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,040
 but there's an incredible video here I encourage you to watch by this

54
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:48,560
 alternative that walks you through all of the things you need to consider.

55
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:51,120
 There are lots of different ways that you can bend and fold metals.

56
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,360
 Probably two of the most common you're likely to come across

57
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:55,440
 are pan breaks and press breaks.

58
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:58,560
 Pan breaks, sometimes called box breaks, are given this name because they

59
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,040
 were classically used to make pans and boxes.

60
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:03,600
 These breaks are reasonably straightforward. They have an upper section

61
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:06,240
 at clamp that you can pull down which holds your material in place

62
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:09,040
 and gives you a nice hard edge to bend against.

63
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:13,360
 And a table which you lift up and as you do brings your material folds your material

64
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:17,120
 upwards. As you can imagine, order of operations is quite important here.

65
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:20,320
 If you fold one side up, you might find that as you go to fold the next,

66
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:23,600
 that side's going to be in the way. It's going to hit the machine as it comes upwards.

67
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:27,120
 For this reason, a lot of box and pan breaks have removable fingers in the clamp

68
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:31,360
 that you can take away to essentially give some room for your previous fold to fit into.

69
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,920
 Box and pan breaks are normally used to fold thinner materials,

70
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:38,400
 anywhere from like one to three mill aluminium and kind of up to one and a half mill steel.

71
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:41,600
 Though the capacity of a particular break is going to be a function of its strength

72
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,360
 and size and stiffness. And usually you'll see stamped on the side of a break,

73
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,760
 it's capacity for both aluminium and steel. For thicker sections or for different materials,

74
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:54,400
 you may turn to a press break rather than a pan break. Here, rather than clamping the material

75
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:58,560
 and bringing a table up to fold it, you put your material over the top of a negative of the

76
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:03,360
 geometry you'd like to form and drive a positive of the geometry you'd like to form down over it.

77
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:06,640
 You can build up reasonably complex shapes using this technique because you can build

78
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,560
 forms that are all sorts of odd shapes to get into little nooks and crannies to be able to do a

79
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:14,000
 fold. And generally speaking, press breaks are driven by hydraulic pumps.

80
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:17,120
 This means that much thicker materials can be folded or bent in a press break

81
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:20,800
 than they can in a pan break. As an interesting aside, it used to be the case that building up a

82
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:24,400
 library of press break tools was reasonably expensive. These days, a lot of people have been

83
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:28,640
 experimenting with 3D printed press break tools to be able to get into odd places and to

84
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:33,040
 impart odd geometries on a shape. You might think that plastics being much softer and much

85
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:36,720
 weaker than steels can't possibly be used to form them, but it turns out that if you make

86
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:40,640
 your geometry just right, if you set your load parts up the correct way, you can fold and

87
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,800
 bend steel with plastic quite easily. And because they're so cheap, 3D printed press break

88
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:48,800
 forms and dies don't need to last very long. So if a low volume runs or where you need to make

89
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,280
 some sort of interesting complicated shape that would otherwise require manufacturing and machining

90
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:57,120
 a whole new die out of metal, these days you can just print to die in all sorts of complicated

91
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:01,440
 geometries. Bending and folding aren't only limited to working on flat sheet or plate,

92
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:05,440
 you can also bend tube and pipe. There are a range of different machines to achieve this task

93
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:08,960
 depending on the size of the tube or pipe you'd like to bend and its wall thickness and the

94
00:05:09,440 --> 00:05:13,520
 material that it's made out of. For reasonably easy jobs like copper pipe, you can probably bend them

95
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:18,080
 by hand, but you can also buy small cute little hand held benders to do the job as well.

96
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:22,000
 For slightly bigger and thicker jobs like say roll bar tubing on a race car, you can invest in

97
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,560
 these floor mount or ratcheting benders that can get a pretty good result as well. When you move up

98
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,640
 to thick boy pipes, you might need to invest in some sort of hydraulic pipe bending machine.

99
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:34,320
 In all cases you need to be a little bit careful when bending pipe and tube products to make sure

100
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:38,720
 that you don't kink the inside wall as you bend them. Sometimes the wall thickness of the pipe is

101
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:42,240
 sufficient to make sure this doesn't happen, but occasionally need to be a little bit careful in

102
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:46,320
 working with thinner sections. Do this you might pack the inside of the pipe full of sand before

103
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:50,640
 you bend it or lightly apply some heat before you bend it or you might insert what are called

104
00:05:50,640 --> 00:05:54,960
 bending balls inside the pipe before you bend it. Bending is a process that doesn't only need to

105
00:05:54,960 --> 00:05:59,520
 be done by hand, CNC bending machines do exist and the extent to which the process of bending is

106
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,520
 computer controlled is very much dependent on the job. Some bending jobs are semi-automated with a

107
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:07,520
 moving back wall for you to put the sheet up against and then the die will go down a fixed

108
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:12,720
 amount so that you can kind of repetitively churn out lots of consistent parts. While other jobs,

109
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:17,360
 for example, the formation of body panels on cars are completely automated. Even at low volume CNC

110
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,720
 bending is quite affordable and like laser cutting there are a bunch of businesses you can send

111
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:24,960
 your jobs to who will quite happily bend your components for a modest fee. And some of these machines

112
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:29,920
 have surprising capability. They can cut and form and bend and punch things into your sheet metal

113
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:34,000
 only in one operation. All of this is to say that complex geometries don't only need to be

114
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,800
 machined or welded or made out of standard parts. You can get reasonably complicated and

115
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:44,240
 custom designs made out of bent components that are reasonably cheap, very strong and stiff and

116
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:49,920
 and quite easy to design and manufacture in reasonably short timeframes.
